Application code that needs to read a row and then write it based on that read should take an explicit lock rather than relying on isolation level alone:

```sql
BEGIN;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 'A' FOR UPDATE;
-- other transactions trying to SELECT ... FOR UPDATE or UPDATE this row now block here
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 'A';
COMMIT;
```

`FOR UPDATE` takes an exclusive-intent lock immediately, so a concurrent transaction attempting the same pattern blocks at its `SELECT`, not later at its `UPDATE` — this is exactly how you close the lost-update gap from a read-then-write pattern. `FOR SHARE` (or `LOCK IN SHARE MODE` in MySQL) takes a shared lock instead — useful when you need to guarantee a row won't change while you read it, but you're not planning to write it yourself.
